<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Middle Ages</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 500–<a href="/years/1500">1500</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> · Also known as medieval period</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Middle Ages is the period of European history between the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-roman-empire">Western Roman Empire</a> in the 5th century and the beginning of the early modern era around 1500. It is usually divided into early, high and late phases. The term is also applied loosely to the same centuries elsewhere, as when histories describe the rise of local dynasties or the abandonment of ancient towns.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/middle-ages.jpg" alt="Middle Ages" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jules &amp; Jenny from Lincoln, UK · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Canterbury_Cathedral,_window_nXV_detail_(46220634195).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Constantinople itself, Europe's largest and richest city for much of the <b>medieval period</b> — perhaps 400,000 to 500,000 people under Justinian, though only around 50,000 by the time of its fall — was the commercial hub connecting the Black Sea, Mediterranean and overland trade systems, and a cultural…</blockquote><a href="/empire/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…and transmitted a large share of the classical Latin texts that survive today, standardised Latin as the language of administration and liturgy in Western Christendom, and created the scribal tradition that kept Roman learning alive through the early <b>medieval period</b> until its fuller recovery in the twelfth-century Renaissance.</blockquote><a href="/empire/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Their ancestors appear in Greek and Roman sources as the Zygoi or Zichoi, and in the <b>Middle Ages</b> the coast was known as Zichia, a land of Byzantine Christian influence where Genoese merchants later kept trading posts such as Matrega and Copa.</blockquote><a href="/empire/circassia">Circassia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The kings of Castile ruled without a fixed capital for most of the <b>Middle Ages</b>, their court moving between Burgos, Toledo, Valladolid, Seville and other cities until Philip II settled it at Madrid in 1561.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crown-of-castile">Crown of Castile · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In the later <b>Middle Ages</b> towns such as Munich, founded by Henry the Lion in 1158, Landshut and Ingolstadt grew under ducal protection.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-bavaria">Duchy of Bavaria · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Its vineyards on the slopes of the Côte d’Or, between Dijon and Beaune, were already prized in the <b>Middle Ages</b>, and monasteries did much to develop them: the monks of Cîteaux built up the walled vineyard of the Clos de Vougeot from the twelfth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-burgundy">Duchy of Burgundy · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
